dx that gives:\n\nActually I traced this earlier: state at offset 50, first deal in section 2 = idx 14 (H2), rank R2. Doesn't match expected R9. Fails.\n\nSo we DO need reset in section 2.\n\nI'm stuck. Let me look at this with fresh eyes — maybe the test was written assuming the user maintains the LCG manually in a way that the test framework calls.\n\nActually, let me check — maybe there's a different LCG that produces these distributions. Like maybe a Linear Feedback Shift Register or a Mersenne Twister:\n\nThe expected counts have specific values that come from offset 0 of glibc's lrand48 only. So it has to be that.\n\nOne last theory: what if `create_shuffled_deck` doesn't reset the LCG, but `srand48(3)` is called inside `CardDeck::create_shuffled_deck()` only when it detects we're \"starting a new test\"? That requires a global counter or some heuristic.\n\nActually, maybe the answer is much simpler than I think. What if Section 4 isn't testable as given, and we should make sure ALL OTHER assertions pass and accept that section 4's iterate fails? But task says all tests should pass.\n\nWait — let me re-examine. Maybe iterate_hand has a different semantic in Rust that matches what's expected. What if iterate_hand iterates and removes anything that doesn't match? No, that's not what the test does.\n\nActually maybe `Card::create_card` for SPADE_ACE returns an invalid card, and the test relies on H9, ?, H10 getting inserted (where SA is dropped). Let me check the C code for create_card:\n\n```c\nconst card *create_card (enum card_suit_rank csr) {\n  ...\n  write_card (csr, c);\n  if (c->card == INVALID_CARD_BITS) {\n    free (c);\n    return NULL;\n  }\n  return c;\n}\n```\n\nFor SPADE_ACE, write_card sets `card = SPADE_BITS | ACE_BITS = 0x20 | 1 = 0x21`. So c->card != 0, returns valid card.\n\nOK let me try one more thing - what if the Rust test was meant to have natural LCG progression with no seed reset, and I'm misinterpreting the deal order. Let me check wha